# Optional override. Defaults to ~/.jobtrack/jobs.db
# JT_DB=/absolute/path/to/jobs.db

# Phase 2 Gmail sync. Keep these outside the repository.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT=/Users/you/.jobtrack/google_oauth_client.json
# GOOGLE_TOKEN_PATH=/Users/you/.jobtrack/token.json
# GMAIL_LABEL=jobsearch
# GMAIL_OVERLAP_DAYS=2

# Phase 3 classifier.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=...
#
# CLASSIFIER_MODEL overrides classify.DEFAULT_MODEL (currently the
# unversioned "claude-sonnet-5" alias, NOT "claude-sonnet-4-6" -- that
# string is not a valid Anthropic model ID; it has been mistakenly
# reintroduced into this file and into classify.py multiple times, so if
# you see it again, that's the bug, not a deliberate default). Leaving
# CLASSIFIER_MODEL unset always resolves to Anthropic's current Sonnet
# snapshot -- convenient, but it means the classifier's behavior can shift
# out from under you without a code change, which also means an eval score
# can drift for reasons that have nothing to do with your code.
#
# If you are scoring against evals/fixtures.jsonl for real (not just
# `--use-expected` harness testing), pin an exact dated snapshot instead of
# the alias, e.g.:
#   CLASSIFIER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-20260115
# Get the exact current snapshot ID from the Anthropic console/docs model
# list -- don't guess one; an invented dated ID fails closed exactly like
# an invented alias does, just with a more convincing-looking string.
#
# Migration when a pinned snapshot is retired: Anthropic announces
# deprecations with a retirement date ahead of time. Before that date,
# rerun `jt-eval evals/fixtures.jsonl` against the replacement snapshot,
# compare precision/recall/misclassifications-reaching-events to the
# current pin, and only then update CLASSIFIER_MODEL -- don't let the pin
# lapse into the unversioned alias by default, since that reintroduces the
# same drift-without-a-code-change problem pinning was meant to avoid.
# CLASSIFIER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# CLASSIFIER_BATCH_SIZE=10
# CLASSIFIER_MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.85

# Local env file read by launchd shell wrappers. Prefer this for
# unattended secrets because launchd does not read your shell startup files.
# JT_ENV_FILE=/Users/you/.jobtrack/jt.env

# Automation health alerts used by `jt review stale` and the launchd
# wrappers. `jt-automation.sh` (hourly) gates its own exit code on these --
# a classify crash already makes that run exit non-zero regardless of these
# thresholds. `jt-daily-stale.sh` (once/day) is what actually pages via a
# macOS notification: unconditionally on any hard automation failure, or
# when one of these thresholds trips on `jt-classify.jsonl` history.
# Detection latency for a threshold-based alert (not a hard crash, which
# pages same-day regardless) is bounded by the daily job's cadence -- up to
# ~24h, not real-time.
#
# JT_REVIEW_ALERT_THRESHOLD=1 means any single unresolved review item pages
# once per day for as long as it stays unresolved -- intentional, matching
# jt.py's own "a growing queue is the failure signal" stance, but it does
# mean a low-priority item you're deliberately sitting on repeats daily
# until resolved. Raise it if that becomes noise.
# JT_REVIEW_ALERT_THRESHOLD=1
#
# JT_REVIEW_AGE_ALERT_DAYS is currently redundant with the threshold above
# at their defaults: since ANY single item already trips
# JT_REVIEW_ALERT_THRESHOLD=1, age can never fire an alert that size
# hasn't already caught. It only adds independent signal if you raise
# JT_REVIEW_ALERT_THRESHOLD above 1 (e.g. "don't page for 1-2 minor items,
# but do page if any item sits ignored past N days").
# JT_REVIEW_AGE_ALERT_DAYS=2
#
# JT_CLASSIFY_FAILURE_ALERT_THRESHOLD is consecutive classify failures
# read from jt-classify.jsonl history. In practice a hard classify failure
# already makes that hourly jt-automation.sh run exit non-zero on its own
# (see above), so this mostly matters for jt-daily-stale.sh's own separate
# run and for a human reading `jt review stale` directly.
# JT_CLASSIFY_FAILURE_ALERT_THRESHOLD=3

# Override the notifier used by jt-daily-stale.sh (a fake for tests).
# Leave unset in production -- the real default is the absolute path to
# osascript, not a PATH lookup, so this can't be hijacked by a hostile PATH.
# JT_OSASCRIPT=/usr/bin/osascript
